Various people said:
>>>>	How can you be blackmailed with something you posted in a public
>>>>forum? If you wouldn't want people who know you to read it...
>>>
>>>Easy.  Imagine a posting to soc.motss, and a professor who doesn't like gays.
>> 
>> 	I repeat - it's a public forum. You have to take responsibility for
>> anything you say in public. 
>
>Fair enough, in theory. In practice, most of everything on Usenet gets
>lost in the noise. Having someone point out to an authority figure that
>a certain conversation is taking place in the babble is a hostile act,
>even if having the conversation was stupid to begin with.

Hostile, perhaps, but blackmail?  I think not.

If a faculty member writes a scathing letter to the local paper,
denouncing the administration of his department/college/university,
why shouldn't it be passed around?  Heck, that 'authority figure' 
might want to write a rebuttal!

If I make a nasty posting about Joe Schmos' ancestry, social skills,
or lack of etiquette, is one of Joe's friends wrong to pass my note
along to Joe?  If I write one of those woman-bashing articles, is it
wrong to pass it on to the UK Women's Forum?

If you toss something out there, you take responsibility for your
views, opinions, and comments.  I fail to see how redistribution of
a Usenet posting (or letter to the editor, or handbill, ...) can be
construed as blackmail.

It seems that most of our administrative discussion end with comments
such as "it's the user's responsibility, not the admins'" and "free
speech!!  free speech!!"  I find it amazing that folks are being accused
of blackmail for actually holding people responsible for statements made 
openly in free speech forums.  (I'd also point out that these have not
(yet) resulted in any unwarranted action against the poster.)  C'mon, 
folks, you can't have it both ways.

--Wes

