From caf-talk Caf Apr 20 20:25:45 1993
From: mlevin@nyx.cs.du.edu (Marshall Levin)
Subject:  Racism at U-Penn?
Message-ID: <1993Apr20.213236.29289@mnemosyne.cs.du.edu>
Date: Tue, 20 Apr 93 21:32:36 GMT


The following is an excerp from a letter sent to me by a friend at U-Penn, who
is a member of the Daily Pennsylvanian Staff:

------------------------------------------------------------------------------


If you read tomorrow's New York Times, or you read USA Today's in brief
section Friday, or The Philadelphia Inquirer on Friday, Sat. or Sunday, or The
Philadelphia Daily News on Friday, or the Washington Post today or saw CNN
this (meaning Sunday) morning, or the Chronicle of Higher Education's next
edition or any Ivy League student newspaper, you may have seen that the Daily
Pennsylvanian and Penn itself are embroiled in minor controversey about racism
and free speach and enforcement of the law.  On Thursday, almost all 14,300
copies of the DP that are normally distributed at sites around campus for free
(we are an independent newspaper with over an $800,000 operating budget
supporting wholly through advertising) were stolen in a highly organized
effort by students claiming to represent "The Black Community."  Members of
Penn's African-American population have been upset about the newspaper's
printing of several columns by Gregory Pavlik, a libertarian,
ultra-conservative right-winger. Pavlik is a regular DP columnist and appears
every other Thursday.  He has in the past written a column questioning Martin
Luther King's status as a national hero based on what Pavlik saw as threats to
property rights found in King's statements and an attack against the
University which he said acted incorrectly by failing to investigate
allegation of hazing at a black student society meeting when such incidents at
predominantly white fraternities are routinely investigated. Pavlik, like all
columnist, was selected to be a columnist by submitting a writing sample - he
was chosen for his writing ability and unique point of view, just like all
other DP columnists - including Maceo Grant, an African-American columnist who
appears on the same page as Pavlik and often has an extremely Afro-centric
agenda.  The DP does not condemn or condone any of the viewpoints of the
columnists who's work appears on its edititorial pages - it simply puts those
ideas out there and helps contribute the market place of ideas so essential to
any University and to this nation's proper functioning.
   Nevertheless, many African-American students were incensed by Pavlik's
columns and wrote a letter to the editor signed by "200 members of Penn's
Black Community" calling the DP racist for printing his work.  The DP
printed their letter along with an editorial contesting the claims and
reiteratting the DP's editorial stance towards its column writers.  Then,
several African-American students filed 30 claims under the University's
racial harassment policies against Pavlik for his work.  The charges were
later dropped by the University's Judicial Inquiry Officer when she
discovered (with the help of several DP staffers who, though they
disagreed with Pavlik, thought he was being persecuted unfairly) that the
University's open expression guidelines, a contract between the University
and the DP, and the First Ammendment all protected Pavlik from being sued
for the content of his columns.
   This apparently was not proper justice for many members of the
African-American community at Penn.  Believing the DP and the University
to be racist, they stole 14,000 DP's in order to draw attention to their
claims and protest Pavlik's final column of the year (which ironically
enough was more or less an advertisement for a new right-wing political
union he was forming at Penn and contained nothing that could be construed
as racist.)  In the course of stealing the papers, about 30 students were
arrested by University police. One student was struck with a short baton
after he resisted arrest.  Several DP photographers captured
African-American students in the act of stealing the papers and there have
been charges and counter-charges of harassment by students stealing the papers
and DP staffers who tried to stop them from doing so.  One DP reporter was
slightly injured when a car used by those stealing the papers ran into him
while he was trying to block its path.  The DP ran a second runnning of
6,000 copies of the paper and distributed them in the afternoon.  The
police released all those they had detained without even taking names
because they chose (incorrectly) to consider taking free papers not a
criminal offense. 
   The DP notified national and local media of the event and of course we
called our attorney (he's a hot-shot libel lawyer from DC). Several
African American students, claiming to represent the amorphous "Black
Community" met with police over the alleged use of excessive measures in
restraining those taking the papers.  Thursday night, the DP office 
assumed a bunker mentality.  The doors were locked, a police
officer and later a private armed security guard were stationed in our
building's lobby, equipment which wasn't needed was stored in locked
closets, and reporters not working on stories for the next day were sent
home. Rumors spread that there was to be a march or a sit-in at the DP
offices that night beginning sometime after 8:00pm and several people
feared a repeat of an incident which occurred at Amherst College earlier
this year when several editors were attacked and much equipment damaged
when angry African-American students, claiming the paper was racist,
raided the offices wielding baseball bats.  We stationed reporters across
the street in safe locations to take pictures should anything
happen.....It turned out nothing did.  The students responsible for
stealing the papers met and decided to march to President Hackney's house
instead.  But, at their supposedly "open" meeting there was more trouble. 
DP reporter swere physically barred from entering even though some
profession media were allowed inside, a clear violation of Pennsylvania's
Sunshine Laws (which say once you let one media organization into an open
forum you must let them all in) and the University's open expression
guidelines. One of our photographers' cameras was damaged when a student
tried to prevent him from taking pictures of the meeting (althoug the
damaging blow was captured on film by another DP photog. who was standing
near-by).  University administrators met in closed emergency session and
came out firmly on (you guessed) both sides - waffling between our rights
to expression and the group of protestors, they said they would protect
our right to publish to the fullest of their abilities, hearing a list of
demands by the protestors, but refusing to call their actions criminal.
  It turns out, their actions were crimianl.  Several precendents exist
where judges have ruled that taking large numbers of free papers with the
intent of preventing their normal distribution represents a criminal
disruption. (The rulings are mostly based on the interstate commerce - free
papers often support themselves through advertisers who engage in
interstate commerce.  Part of that commerce involves advertising in
publications. By stealing the papers, the thiefs have interfered with the
commerce of the businesses advertising in the paper.  There are other
rulings that state that stealing free papers amounts to de facto censorship.)
    The University is still waffling.  The African-American students are
sticking to their guns (and in my opinion setting themselves up to get the
shit sued out of them by the paper which lost thousands in advertising
revenue and in the cost of reprinting 6,000 copies of the paper on short
notice.)  The University is getting lots of negative press.  Racial
tensions are growing.  Gregory Pavlik is alternately being portrayed by
the national media as a Nazi or as the next William F. Buckley (some might
thing this is the same thing).  It is also very interesting.  Penn
President Hackney is in trouble because he has recently been named
Clinton's pick to head the National Endowment for the Humanities and faces
a potentially rough Senate confirmation as this incident calls into
question his reputation as "upholder of free expression" he has earned
over the last 12 years as Penn's pres.  

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
