Or would it?  I've expanded the newsgroups addressed with
this posting to intercept the readers of a number of recent
threads I see connected to this topic.  sci.econ-omists,
for example, have recently been discussing the Physics
community's great success with electronic (pre-)publication
of research articles, and what the costs and benefits of
such an approach would be for Economists.  Mathematicians
and engineers are considering what will be the future of
their professional societies as fewer and fewer subscribe
for printed copies of journals, and reduce the (excess)
revenues that now go to subsidize society activities.
Right-thinking observers of the academic scene realize
that the US "publish or perish" academic culture is over-
due for a shakeout, probably to be precipitated by a
taxpayer revolt (perhaps under a different label).

In my experience, intellectual achievement appears as
the epiphenomena of publication in refereed journals,
invitations to address professional conferences, receipt
of grants and awards, and so on.  Whether we like it or
not, global imperatives of miniaturization and noumenali-
zation will lead to a world which recognizes and rewards
those who successfully ride light-speed wavefronts.  What
will it mean when being a net.god counts for more than
getting tenure?  How close are we to that time?  Who is
making plans on how to make a living when ideas flow from
one scholar to the next without pausing to be printed on
paper?  What if Usenet *is* the (academic) community at
large?

Please direct follow-ups appropriately.
