Here are some replies from universities and technical institutes in the
U.S. and Australia.

*============== A state university ================

we rarely receive complaints.  in general we acknowledge non-local
complaints with a simple ``we'll look into it'' although we might be
more specific if there does appear to be something which might suggest
someone is attempting to represent our institution in some fashion.  if
the posting in question is exhorting some community of users to direct
a specific network based ``attack'' on a user we will contact the user
and remind them that there are acceptable use provisions associated
with network access or that there are terms of use associated with a
local account.  this has happened once in the last four years.  we
don't have the time, inclination or authority to involve ourselves in
what appears to be the vast, overwhelming majority of such cases that
we see on usenet.

we do have a large collection of local newsgroups, many of which are
for class-room support.  although we haven't had to deal with it we
would treat a disruption in such a newsgroup the same way we (the
institution) would treat such a disruption in a class.  we do have
newsgroups explicitly for open discussion.


*============= A state university =====

the university has policies on use and abuse of computers.  Whenever
we get a message from the outside world about our users we do 1 of 2 things

1) report the incident to their supervising professor (if they are a 
grad student)

2)  report them to the student discipline office.  

They are contacted and usually stop their actions.  If they persist,
we make a formal meeting with them citing the INTERNET usage policy
which is for academic and research purposes.  If they can't adhere to
the INTERNET policy, we find a way to deny them INTERNET access (ie
they can t post to news)

========================== A follow up to the query: ==================
"Can you send me a copy of your internet policy? 
Do you forbid offensive expression?"
=======================================================================

I am not a lawyer and none of my views represent what [XXXX] has for 
policies.  In fact, [XXXX] does NOT have a formal Usenet
usage policy.  In fact, [XXXX] (as far as I can tell) has never
endorsed supporting Usenet.  It is still in the "TEST" phase
(and has been for over 4 years). 

I belive [XXXX] would turn off the service if someone went to so
far as to fight a 1st amendment charge.  I believe that [XXXX] would
site lack of funding and cost of bandwidht as reasons for
removing the service.

What we do is talk to the individual.   They usually cooperate without
further incident.  If it continues, we turn it over to the student
discipline officer who then applies general [XXXX] student regulations
which each student agress to follow while at [XXXX].  The student
could then challenge the decision, but then they have to fight the
university lawyer, etc.

For a staff member at [XXXX], I guess they would receive a formal
reprimand from their superior for not doing [XXXX] stuff or not
following [XXXX] guidelines.  They of course could challenge that too.

Basically, we have no policy.  we have an informal mechanism that
seems to work.....




Now, for my interpretation of stuff...


Since Use net goes across the backbone, then local policy must adhere to 
NFSnet policies whenever their activities use the backbone (which is 
ALL the time when it comes to Usenet).


The acceptable use policy (items 1-8) stress the professional
development aspects of the net.  It stresses research and grant 
development.

Usenet is public forum, hence any communications using that vehicle should
fall under the "professional" development portion of NFSnet.   I believe
a person should follow "professional" form when using this vehicle.
If a communication is questionable, then a group of designated
people representing the local corporation should make an evaluation.  If
the communication is determined to be unacceptable, then the person
should stop using NFSnet as their vehicle for communication.

(I am proposing a peer review committee at each local site).

Of course, a person could argue that their communication was
perfectly fine and the peer group was not a representative group
of professionals.

Then maybe, a new newsgroup could be formed polling people on the
specific communication.  A vote could be held.  If a majority
of people feel that it is a professional comminication, then so
be it.  If not, the person should formally apologise or
have no Usenet facilities.

Again, NFSnet activities is for research and professional development.
If a majority of professionals feel that a person is misusung the
net, then they probaly are.......


*=========== A state university ===========

I usually stay out of it unless the person here is really out of line.  In
that case I ask them to tone it down and they usually do.  We haven't had
too many complaints (<5) about our users.


*==================== A State University ==========
Usenet news is administered by a student group.  I one of the
staff members that gets copies of all email to usenet@[XXXX - cmk]
I'm on that list so that I'll see any complaints that come in.
We believe that anyone that sends in a complaint should receive
a response.  I take it as one of my responsibilities to make
sure that happens.

We do not "regularly receive demands to punish..." users for what
they post.  Of the few complaints received, most seem to run
afoul of other rules.  For example, I've received two complaints
about commercial use of the "for sale" lists.  Whether or not
this violates Usenet rules, commerical use of a student computer
account (sale of stuff for profit) is outside the scope of what
we allow students to do with their accounts.  The wording our
our policy statement is probably too fuzzy here: we prohibit
use for "personal gain" where perhaps we should have said
"personal financial gain".  In one of the two cases, we simply
told the person to stop running his mail order business through
University computers posto hasto.  He complied.  In the other
case, we talked to the student and determined that he was not
engaged in an activity for personal gain and sent a reply saying
that back to the complainer.

We have received a complaint from someone who was mad at a poster
to alt.sex.something.  The complaintant pointed out that the
person posting was using someone elses' account -- the posting
in question said as much.  I followed up by contacting the
account owner of record by telephone.  She denied any knowledge
that an account had been opened in her name.  We closed the
account at her request.  I sent a letter to her address of
record confirming our phone conversation.  

Earlier this year, I got a complaint about an undergraduate from
a faculty member: "Who does this guy think he is, anyway?  Can
you shut him up?"  The student was simply mouthing off about
something that he arguably knew nothing about.  My reply to
the faculty member was that no one that works for us has it in
their job description to protect faculty members from misstatements
or misquotes of the undergrads.  He would have to deal with it
himself.

An area where we haven't had any trouble for several years is
harassment.  This isn't specific to Usenet news, of course.
Perhaps because I'm uncomfortable in this area, or maybe because
I think it's "none of my business", our computer rules simply
say, in effect, that University policies regarding harrassment
apply to computer communications (email, usenet news).  We
leave the definition of harrassment to the "experts" that wrote
a general harassment policy that is part of the University Rule
Book.  Somewhere around here is a draft of "helpful suggestions"
written to someone that is being harrassed telling them the
steps that they can take and the proper office to contact if
informal resolution doesn't work or isn't appropriate.  

Finally, I've got a draft paragraph that I've proposed for inclusion
in the student rule book:

>Section [XXX] of the student rule book deals generally with 
>posters, announcements and banners.  The current sections are:
>
>  a.  posting in the colleges
>  b.  posting on non-college bulletin boards
>  c.  bus shelters and kiosks
>  d.  postings must include ID of responsible party and removal date
>
>We propose a new section:
>
>  e.  University students, faculty and staff with computer logins
>      may post non-commercial messages and announcements through
>      [XXX]'s electronic news server.  Users are expected to familiarize
>      themselves with Usenet ettiquette and rules before posting.
>      All responsibility for statements made in electronic news messages 
>      lies with the person posting the message who therefore should
>      not take steps that would hide his/her identity.


*=================== A state university   ==================

Never had such a complaint and hope I never do.  The worst problem I've
had with news was the famous [XXXX] controversy.  The user here was
basically inthe right.  The fellow at [XXX] that we had problems with
was a nut case and the issue got resolved pretty well by the cabal.

If we were to get a complaint my reaction would depend on the nature of
the complaint, its source, and who it was directed at.  If there seemed to
be serious merit to the complaint and the excahnge in news was ongoing
then the login would probably be inactivated until the user and I had a
chance for a face to face talk about it.

The major problem that we have here is sharing of accounts and people
taking over inactive accounts.  Disabling the login takes care of this
kind of thing pretty quickly and that is why it is my favored approach to
misuse of computer logins.

The closest thing that we have had to the sort of thing that interests you
is a case of a student here sending mail to a former girl friend at
another school.  He cur it out as soon as we had a little talk.  He didn't
consider what he was doing to be harassment.  It pretty clearlay looked
like harassment to the recipient.

* ============== A State University =============

I have only received about 5 such requests in the five years we have
had usenet. In each case, I have passed the complaint on to my
supervisor for review.  In general we inform the person that a
complaint has been received, but we take no action unless some law or
university policy has been violated.  In the most recent case, we
informed the user in question that his actions seemed to conflict with
the university policy on racial and ethnic harrasment and asked the he
be more careful in the future.  In one case several years ago, one of
our employees at the computer center posted a chain letter to several
groups. He was informed by his supervisor that this was not an
appropriate use of his work account, and he was asked to cancel all
the articles posted and post an apology, which he did. To my knowlege,
no account has ever been revoked or any formal disiplinary action
taken as a result of usenet on our campus.

