Obviously, what's happened is that, when I said that your posting *might*
violate University policy, someone took that to mean that we were taking
action against you.  

Of course my personal opinion is that we're all better served by keeping
Net conversations constructive and avoiding abusive language.

*===================== A private university ==========

Each case of system abuse (or at least reported system abuse) is handled
in an individualized manner.  Therefore, it would be difficult for me to
give you a "crime -> punishment" listing.  I will go through our typical
pocess for handleing these cases.

- A complaint comes in to either postmaster (typically external
    users), root, or advisor (typically internal users) that someone is
    violating some policy.  (I'll talk about these later).

- If the complaint in question includes the appropriate information,
    then a response to the effect of "thanks for letting us know about
    this, I've forwarded your complaint to the appropriate people." 
    This is typically the last we talk to the original complaintant
    (That is, it is not our practice to let anyone else know what, if
    any, action has been taken.  That's private.).

- The message is then forwarded off to the appropriate people.  This
    is a small group of us who handle abuse cases.  We then review the
    information given to us (as well as any relevant information we
    collect ourselves) and make a decision on what to do.  Punishment
    can range from a warning letter, to removal of external posting
    privilages.  Extreme cases can result in reporting the incident to
    the Dean of Student Affairs (who can do things like suspend or fine
    the individual).

Policies:  Our policies on electronic communication are basically the
standard NSFnet requirements (no commercial sollicitation, no chain
letters, limit cross-posting, no abusive language) plus our [XXXX]-wide
policy of communication in general (no threatening people's lives with
chainsaws).

========= Followup =============================

> Can you give me more details about what "no abuse language"
> entails? Does it cover public postings (as contrasted with
> person-to-person email)?

The abusive language clause includes both private and public postings. 
Unlike chain letters, we take no action on messages with swearing or
threats unless we receive a complaint about it.  Context really
> determines the extent of the problem.

> Also, what is the due process procedure for removing
> external posting privileges? Can the accused request a hearing?
> How long are the privileges suspended? Is the accused told
> how to appeal, etc.

Losing posting privileges is an extreme case.  It has never been given
to a first time offender.  That is, if a particular user has shown a
pattern of abuse and we have explored other possabilites first, then we
may resort to this possability.  Any time we take any action, there is a
two-way dialog with the user.  That is, they can come speak with us in
person or send us email (whichever they choose).  A decision is rarely
reached without giving the user a chance to defend himself or herself. 
(For example, chain letters are rarely arguable).  The length of any
punishment is determined by the severity of the abuse.

These answers might not be as concrete as you would like, but our system
is designed to be fair and flexable.  We make every attempt to let the
user explain the situation and gather as much information as possible in
order to come to a reasonable decision.

*==================== A private university ==============

We get several of these complaints a year.  There's no form letter as
luckily they're not that frequent, and they tend to be different
anyway.  Our official policy statement for accounts is attached at the
end of this message.  There isn't any rule specific to netnews, so
the policy involved is more general.

We (at ["Computer Services Department" -cmk]) would like to give users
as much freedom as possible to post what they want.  When we get a
complaint, we tell the user that a complaint was made.  Almost all
complaints are violations of netiquette like excessive cross-posting,
posting to an irrelevant newsgroup, using vulgarities (in groups that
normally do not!), and so forth.  We advise the user what would have
been a wiser course of action, in the interests of making netnews work
better, but this isn't "punishment".  Most such cases are a question
of the user not knowing any better.

I don't know what high schools are like these days, but many
undergraduate users seem to feel that any word we give is a "warning"
and that they've been "punished".  Likewise they expect that when they
see something they don't like on netnews, "punishment" is called for.
I feel I've disappointed them sometimes.  Pointers to articles in
news.announce.newusers are sometimes in order.

In some instances, obscene or pointless messages are a sign that a
terminal was left unattended, or a password shared or cracked.  Where
this appears to be the case, we will prevent login until we see the
owner of the account.  This is not punishment for the messages as
such, but system security.  In certain cases, complaints based on a
netnews posting will point to this kind of problem.

In a recent complaint, someone felt a [university] poster had sent a message to
inappropriate newsgroups.  It wasn't a long message.  A couple of us
differed in our opinions about the choice of newsgroups.  We told the
poster someone had complained, advised using judgement as to when to
cross-post, but told him the case in point was just borderline.  The
complainer wanted stronger action, but we saw no basis.

In another recent complaint, a user outside [XXX] reported an obscene
posting (on a newsgroup that does not normally contain them).  The
posting was never provided and it had expired here, so I don't really
know what was in it.  The complainer had exchanged flame mail with the
poster, both threatening to tell the other's system administrator if
the other did not stop sending mail.  The complainer also said the
posting had violated federal laws.  My first reply was in full:

> I have here a message from [complainer] quoting from mail sent by
> each of you to the other, and I don't know what you're arguing about,
> which should indicate how substantive the discussion is.  May I
> suggest that the two of you simply stop sending mail to each other, or
> delete it without reading it, and move on to better things?  I don't
> even care who started it.

In an exchange that ran on longer than I anticipated, I sent
increasingly shorter messages, including:

> I still think deleting mail from him is a simple solution.

He continued to refer to the Secret Service and federal law.  I said:

> Vulgarity is not against the law.  Sorry.  Any further discussion of
> legality should be done between attorneys.

I was itching to ask what law this would be, but even if he'd told me,
I wasn't about to go the Law library and study cases, so my last reply
in full was:

> I'm not going to argue points of law.

[...]


*============== A private university =============
	So far I haven't gotten any messages asking me to do so.  My
tendence would be to decline any such request suggesting that perhaps they
should ignore postings from this user.  If we were talking about abusive
email, I might be more inclined to get involved; but even then I don't think
I would do anything more then request that the individual stop the behavior.


*================== A public technical institute ===================

