Personally, I'm a First Amendment fan - "if you don't like it, don't read/look
at it". We *do* have some PC types on campus, so I expect some sort of 
censorship will be coming down the pike eventually...

} I know there is great interest in the question of how best to respond
} to such demands. Sharing this information is a way to starting
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

I try to ignore them - we *are* talking about adults here, right? :^)

*================== A private techical institute ==

Fortunately this does not happen to us often.  When it does we usually
write up a letter explaining that we do not make a policy of censoring
the postings of our users and suggest that the complainer learn to use
kill files.

If the local user does some egregiously rude thing like cancel other
people's messages or flood a newsgroup with lots of messages or
bombard someone with email we will take action ranging from asking
them nicely not to do that to disabling their account (if it is an
account over which we have control).

========== Follow up =====

We are total anarchy.  Users who "abuse system resources" tend to get
notified several times via email before we get around to really doing
anything.  Short term disabling of accounts is used (very
infrequently) as a way to get a response from users who don't respond
to email.  Our organization has the right to maintain the cluster and
the campus network as we see fit to meet AUP of our regional net and
keep users on the cluster from taking advantage of each other.  We
have the option of referring latter such problems to the Undergraduate
and Graduate organizations who enforce the Honor code and determine if
an infringement has been made and what kind of restitution can be
made.

*===== A large private university ===
I generally forward the complaint to the system administrator
of the originating machine and I notify the complainant of my
action and give them the direct address.

Many times, people complain directly to the system administrator
and I do not get involved, since I only deal with the news server.

If the complaints persist I will forward them to the department
head or computing center head in that department.  So far,
it's never gotten that far.

The system administrator calls the person in and first asks
if it was him/her that did the posting.  If the answer is "no"
an attempt is made to figure out who did.  Once the person is
identified, he/she gets a lecture from the system administrator,
and the abuse usually stops.  If the abuse continues the
matter usually gets turned over to various overseeing boards
which enforce University harrassment policy and any other
applicable policies (like electronic name forging).

If the person is a guest on the server (NNTP read/post access) and
not affiliated with the University, I give them one warning and
remove them from the server if it happens again.  (I don't
appreciate being nice and then getting hassled for it.)

I don't have any sample replies handy, since each situation
is usually different.  But the main point here is that as the
server operators we don't police our users.

[...]

Of course, any posting which is free speech and not harrassment is
protected.  Differences in opinion are expected.

*========== A private, religious university ======

   Actually, I haven't ever had a problem with this. Thus we have no
set policy. If we did have one, I have the feeling it would be less
than enlightened, as our news reading policy is becoming. But as we
aren't that big of a news posting site (more of a news passing site),
I don't see this becoming a problem.

*=============== An Australian university ===================
We deal with this problem by not allowing it to happen in the first
place.  Our undergraduate student population has restricted Usenet (and
indeed Internet) access, and limited posting capabilities.  Postgraduate
students are assumed to be responsible enough to have full computing
access and so can read and post all they like. 

I have never received a request to "punish a user" for posting to
Usenet. 

*============ An Australian university ====
We have a set of "usage guidelines" for email which also
applies to news. If some breaches the guidelines they have their
account removed for some time period.
If it appears fairly harmless they just get a warning.

If it was a complaint
about behaviour that does not breach our guidleines, I don't know
what we would do.

It hardly ever happens. I think we have had about
3 cases in 3 years.

