dz>[...] The policy is that they can suspend accounts before a
dz>determination of wrongdoing. 

ck> I agree that restoration is more important, but think "no 
ck> punishment pending final action" is also important.

Since so much computer wrongdoing is caused not by the account owner, but
by an account cracker or account sharer, that we suspend accounts here at
Penn Engineering before a determination of wrongdoing, too.  So, a student
might go from Friday evening until Monday morning without access to a
computer account.  As soon as the student shows a Penn ID and signs a copy
of the policy to indicate that s/he has received it, the account is
restored.  Depending on the situation, we may require that the account
password be changed at that time also.

Our justification for doing that is:
(1) to protect the files in the user's account, usually homework and
papers in progress, from cracking,
(2) to prevent a cracker from sending out messages and postings from
the user's account, and
(3) to ensure that the user is aware of the computer policy.

Advantages to doing that are:
(1) Users who, for whatever reason, were unaware that a computer
policy is enforced, tend to be more careful in their future 
behavior.  We usually don't bring judicial actions against first
offenders, and users usually stop violating the policy after a
first warning.
(2) In case of a judicial action for a future violation, we have 
a record that the user was aware of the policy.

If a student is unable to complete an assignment on time because
of our action, we will explain the situation to the professor and
help make sure that the student is not penalized. 

We have been doing this for two years, and it has worked very well.

-Helen Anderson
Penn Engineering

