[Article written in 1991]
PROFESSOR CRITICIZES UC-BERKELEY DISCRIMINATORY ADMISSIONS POLICY
 Radical Students Charge Vincent Sarich of Being "Racist" and
 "Sexist"

  The theories of a professor at the University of California at
 Berkeley have provoked discussion about the limits of academic
 freedom and the controversial topic of race, as well as indicted
 the concept of _political correctness_ that has permeated many
 campuses.
  Vincent Sarich, a professor of anthropology at Berkeley, first
 encountered the controversy when he authored a commentary in
 California Monthly, the University of California alumni magazine,
 contemplating the university_s attempts to racialy diversify the
 student body through the use of quotas.  Sarich wrote that the
 program _systematically, and increasingly discriminated against
 white students and institutionalized racism on our campus._
  The UC-Berkeley administration has adopted policies that are
 meant to make the representation of the student population reflect
 the racial make-up of the state of California.  Starting next
 year, only 40% of the incoming freshmen will be selected solely on
 the basis of academic merit.
  Sarich wrote: _[Berkeley_s] eminence obviously cannot be
 maintained if race, sex, ethnicity, or any other factors are
 allowed to substitute for achievement.  There cannot possibly be,
 if we wish to maintain that eminence, a performance-independent
 right to membership in the university community....You get
 performance by rewarding performance.  You insure non-performance
 by rewarding non-performance._
  In response to the statements, between 50 and 75 students calling
 themselves the _Ad Hoc Committee Against Sarich_ stormed Sarich_s
 classroom as he lectured the over 400 student enrolled in his
 _Physical Anthropology_ course.  Members of the _committee_
 gathered behind the podium from which Sarich was lecturing and
 accused him of teaching _racist_ and _sexist_ material.  Despite
 attempts to continue his lecture, Sarich was soon forced to cancel
 the class.
  Students enrolled in the class were reportedly angered by the
 protesters and accused them of infringing upon Sarich_s right to
 speak.
  Protest leader Daniel Jerome was quoted in The Daily Californian
 saying the class _is the consolidation of fascism.  He_s [Sarich]
 justifying his sh-t with fake pseudo-science bullsh-t._  Toby
 Douglas, a student in the class, disagreed, saying, _I think [the
 disruption] is ignorance because they think we_re being
 brainwashed.  We_re here to listen to different viewpoints._
  Sarich later told The Washington Times that he was _sort of proud
 of [his] class.  They stayed there and took [the protesters] on in
 energetic discussion._  He noted that many of the students were
 _ticked off_ by charges that they were being _brainwashed._  He
 told The Daily Californian: _Very few of those protesters are in
 my class.  Very few of them are familiar with the material._
  Commenting on support for his actions from the faculty following
 the incident, Sarich said, _Maybe some of my colleagues learned
 their lessons [from the 1960s] too well.  Although I_ve gotten a
 large number of communications from my colleagues supportive of
 what I had to say, I haven_t heard any of them come out in public,
 so they_re scared [to voice their support]._
  UC-Berkeley later condemned the incident, releasing a statement
 that read: _The attempt to silence opinions by the disruption of a
 class is a clear violation of the freedom of a teacher to teach
 and of students to learn._
  The controversy over Sarich, however, did not end with his
 California Monthly commentary.  Sarich later found himself
 defending his theories on behavioral development against an
 onslaught of fellow professors and students during a debate in a
 dormitory dining hall.
  Sarich has stated that race and ethnicity can be correlated with
 academic and athletic performance when statistical averages are
 brought into consideration.  Similar theories from Professor
 Philippe Rushton at the University of Western Ontario prompted
 calls for his removal from the school_s faculty and orders from
 the administration to give his lectures by videotape to prevent
 protests of his lectures (see Campus Report, April and September,
 1990).
  Sarich told The Daily Californian that the relationship of the
 brain size and skull capacity correlate with _some aspects of
 intellectual functioning._  Noting that the human brain has
 tripled in size over two million years along with, _we believe,_
 human intelligence, he says _the only way that could have happened
 was if people with larger brains were advantaged over people with
 smaller brains._
  Sarich further enraged many by saying that _heterosexuality is
 more natural_ than homosexuality.  He has noted that few cultures
 have a substantial amount of homosexuality, and has also said:
 _There can_t be a gene for homosexuality_how the hell would it
 perpetuate itself?_
  During the debate, fellow Professor of Anthropology Nancy
 Scheper-Hughes questioned the integrity of Sarich_s theories,
 saying, _what is being presented in Sarich_s class isn_t science,
 it isn_t bad science, it isn_t science at all.  These are
 impressionable minds that are being tampered with._
  Commenting on the situation at Berkeley, the San Francisco
 Examiner, while acknowledging their disagreement with Sarich,
 wrote: _We believe that there is a grave danger in preventing
 people from speaking because they have divergent political
 views....Ironically, a generation ago, activists at Berkeley
 created the Free Speech Movement.  Today, they march in the
 anti-Free Speech Movement.  The earlier group had it right._
  Speaking on the campus racial climate at Berkeley, Daily
 Californian columnist Max Boot noted the increasing trend of
 racial divisiveness as the _Balkanization of Berkeley._  In one of
 his columns, Boot attacks the notions of "P.C.," otherwise known
 as "politically correct."  Boot notes: "When they speak of
 diversity on campus, the 'politically correct' are referring to
 racial, not ideological, diversity.  When they preach against the
 traditional canons of Western civilization, it is 'white culture'
 that they rail against.  When they ask for the admission of the
 underprivileged to UC-Berkeley, they are referring not to the poor
 but to Hispanics, blacks, and Native Americans. "
  Boot claims: _Once upon a time, the United States was a _melting
 pot_ where the descendants of Russian serfs, African slaves, and
 English shopkeepers were united by a common language and culture.
 Not anymore.  _P.C-ness_ holds that Western culture is oppressive
 and should not be taught at UC-Berkeley.  They sneer at the
 old-fashioned idea of _assimilation.__

