In article <1993Apr22.164847.3521@news.columbia.edu> dan@cubmol.bio.columbia.edu (Daniel Zabetakis) writes:

> >Yes. But policy making and system/policy administering are not
> >identical. At a university, at least ideally, everyone has
> >a voice in policy making. 

>    Sure. Except that administrators are expected to make up rules for
> thier particular area. It's an open question as to whether they would
> be responsive to campus opinion.

Administrators are IMHO those people who have to run the systems and
care about the rules for running the systems being obeyed. They are
the "executive branch", just like the librarians (I assume that
usually the policies of an University library is set by the
University's various governing bodies, not by the library staff
exclusively.) 

>    In almost every case, people don't want a voice in policy making. Mostly
> it is due to lack of interest. I don't care what hours the art labs are open.
> In the same way, art students don't care how you get access to the animal
> care facilities.

That people have no interest in what they don't have a need for, is
rather obvious, or isn't it? Those students who don't use the
computing facilities at all don't need to care about its policies, but
those who use it should have a natural interest in them. For example,
whether they get network access or what the opening hours are is
relevant to them.

People who really don't care although they better did are common too,
but this is no excuse to give those who want no rights at all. IMHO
students should have rights of participation in all matters which are
of importance to them.

>   Adding "We are open to input from the university community" would be just
> senseless extra wordage.

Surely it would be, if the people who issue this are the same who will
happily throw said input into the trash without further consideration.
But mostly you will *get* input if you ask for and show that you
really care about it.

Olaf
